    <div class="section-heading">Background Information</div>

    <div style="margin-top: 20px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify;">Agarose gel electrophoresis is a widely used procedure in various areas of biotechnology. This simple, but precise, analytical procedure is used in molecular biology to separate DNA fragments based on their size. The technique is a convenient analytical method for determining the size of DNA molecules. The gel matrix acts as a sieve, allowing smaller fragments to move faster through the pores than larger ones. This principle is used to separate charged biological molecules such as DNA, RNA and proteins.</p>
    </div>

    <div style="margin-top: 30px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify;">The centrifuge and "workhorse" of agarose gel electrophoresis is the horizontal gel electrophoresis apparatus. There are many types of horizontal gel electrophoresis apparatus, but the most common are those that are designed to run gels in a horizontal orientation. Other types, such as protein (or vertical) electrophoresis, may utilize an electric field to drive the migration of charged molecules through a gel matrix. The horizontal electrophoresis apparatus is essentially a sophisticated electrical circuit that allows for the application of a constant voltage across the gel. The apparatus contains platinum electrodes because of platinum's superior electrical conductivity and permanence. Because platinum electrodes are expensive and fragile, care should be taken when handling electrophoresis equipment.</p>
    </div>

    <div style="margin-top: 30px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify;">The separation medium is a gel made from agarose, which is a polysaccharide derived from seaweed. Agarose is highly purified to remove impurities and charges. It is derived from the same source as gelatin, but it is much more stable and is used in a food product called agar agar, which is used to prepare a gelatin-like dessert. Agarose is a polymer that forms a gel when it is heated and then allowed to cool. The gel matrix acts as a sieve, separating molecules based on their size. In EDVOTEK experiments, the agarose is mixed with hydrocolloids which help to form a stable gel. The gel is then poured into a casting tray, which serves as a mold. A well-former template (often called a comb) is placed across the end of the casting tray to form wells where the gel solution solidifies.</p>
    </div>

    <div style="margin-top: 30px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify;">After the gel solidifies, the gel is submerged in a buffer-filled electrophoresis chamber which contains a positive electrode at one end, and a negative electrode at the other. The buffer conducts the electrical current through the gel. The electrical current drives the migration of the charged molecules through the gel matrix. DNA molecules are negatively charged, so they migrate towards the positive electrode (anode). Proteins, which can have various charges depending on the buffer pH, may migrate towards the negative electrode (cathode) or may not migrate at all. The separation of DNA fragments is based on their size. Smaller fragments can move through the pores of the gel matrix more easily and therefore migrate faster than larger fragments. This differential migration rate is what allows for the separation of DNA fragments.</p>
    </div>

    <div style="margin-top: 30px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify;">If electrophoresis is conducted using dye samples, the migration of the various colored molecules through the gel matrix can be visualized directly in the gel during electrophoresis and do not require staining. The dye molecules are designed to migrate at a rate similar to that of the DNA fragments, allowing for visualization of the separation process. However, the size of the dye molecules also makes them susceptible to separation during the electrophoresis process.</p>
    </div>

    <div style="margin-top: 30px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify;">In EDVOTEK experiments, the agarose is mixed with hydrocolloids which help to form a stable gel. The gel is then poured into a casting tray, which serves as a mold. A well-former template (often called a comb) is placed across the end of the casting tray to form wells where the gel solution solidifies. The gel is then placed into an electrophoresis chamber filled with buffer. The chamber contains a positive electrode at one end, and a negative electrode at the other. The electrical current drives the migration of the charged molecules through the gel matrix. DNA molecules are negatively charged, so they migrate towards the positive electrode (anode). Proteins, which can have various charges depending on the buffer pH, may migrate towards the negative electrode (cathode) or may not migrate at all. The separation of DNA fragments is based on their size. Smaller fragments can move through the pores of the gel matrix more easily and therefore migrate faster than larger fragments. This differential migration rate is what allows for the separation of DNA fragments.</p>
    </div>

    <div style="position: absolute; bottom: 30px; right: 60px; font-size: 10pt;">Page 4</div>
</div>

<div class="page">
    <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: center; margin-bottom: 30px;">
        <div style="font-size: 12pt;">EDVO-Kit # {{ kit_number }}</div>
        <div style="font-size: 12pt;">Principles and Practice of Agarose Gel Electrophoresis</div>
        <div style="font-size: 12pt;">Page 5</div>
