    <div class="section-heading">Agarose Gel Electrophoresis</div>

    <div style="margin-top: 20px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify;">Sturdiier and more resilient, and consequently are less prone to breakage than conventional agarose. The enhanced resolving power and translu-cency of UltraSpec-Agarose&trade; results in greater visual clarity and definition of separated DNA fragments after staining.</p>
    </div>

    <div style="margin-top: 30px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify;">The gel is made by dissolving agarose powder in boiling buffer solution. The solution is then cooled to approximately 55&deg;C and poured into a casting tray which serves as a mold. A well-former template (often called a comb) is placed across the end of the casting tray to form wells where the gel solution solidifies.</p>
    </div>

    <div style="margin-top: 30px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify;">After the gel solidifies, the gel is submerged in a buffer-filled electrophoresis chamber which contains a positive electrode at one end, and a negative electrode at the other. The buffer conducts the electrical current through the gel. The electrical current drives the migration of the charged molecules through the gel matrix. DNA molecules are negatively charged, so they migrate towards the positive electrode (anode). Proteins, which can have various charges depending on the buffer pH, may migrate towards the negative electrode (cathode) or may not migrate at all. The separation of DNA fragments is based on their size. Smaller fragments can move through the pores of the gel matrix more easily and therefore migrate faster than larger fragments. This differential migration rate is what allows for the separation of DNA fragments.</p>
    </div>

    <div style="position: absolute; bottom: 30px; right: 60px; font-size: 10pt;">Page 5</div>
</div>

<div class="page">
    <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: center; margin-bottom: 30px;">
        <div style="font-size: 12pt;">EDVO-Kit # {{ kit_number }}</div>
        <div style="font-size: 12pt;">Principles and Practice of Agarose Gel Electrophoresis</div>
        <div style="font-size: 12pt;">Page 6</div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-heading">Agarose Gel Electrophoresis</div>

    <div style="margin-top: 20px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify;">On the other hand, gels separating DNA require staining in order to be visualized. Although DNA samples that are prepared for electrophoresis typically appear blue-purple, the DNA itself does not have color. The color comes from a dye in a gel loading solution that is added at the end of the gel preparation process. The dye molecules migrate through the gel at a rate similar to that of the DNA fragments, allowing for visualization of the separation process. In general, most DNA samples follow behind the tracking dye during electro-phoresis. This is important that electrophoresis is terminated before the tracking dye runs off the end of the gel.</p>
    </div>

    <div style="margin-top: 30px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify;">The most commonly used stains for visualizing DNA contain either ethidium bromide or methylene blue. Ethidium bromide is a mutagen and must be handled with extreme care. Methylene blue is a less toxic alternative. Visualization also requires a short wave ultraviolet light source (transilluminator) to excite the dye molecules, which then emit visible light. The dye molecules are incorporated into the gel matrix during the gel preparation process. The dye molecules are designed to migrate at a rate similar to that of the DNA fragments, allowing for visualization of the separation process.</p>
    </div>

    <div style="margin-top: 30px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify;">Agarose gel electrophoresis possesses great resolving power, yet is rela-tively simple and straightforward to perform. The agarose gel consists of a matrix of pores through which the DNA molecules migrate. The pores are created by the gel matrix, and the size of these pores determines the resolving power of the gel. The size of the pores is determined by the concentration of the agarose in the gel. Higher concentrations of agarose result in smaller pores, which allows for better separation of smaller DNA fragments. The mobility of molecules during electrophoresis is also influenced by gel concentration, buffer pH, and the applied voltage. The gel concentration and the volume to maximize successful experimental results.</p>
    </div>

    <div style="position: absolute; bottom: 30px; right: 60px; font-size: 10pt;">Page 6</div>
</div>

<div class="page">
    <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: center; margin-bottom: 30px;">
        <div style="font-size: 12pt;">EDVO-Kit # {{ kit_number }}</div>
        <div style="font-size: 12pt;">Principles and Practice of Agarose Gel Electrophoresis</div>
        <div style="font-size: 12pt;">Page 7</div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-heading">Agarose Gel Electrophoresis</div>

    <div style="margin-top: 20px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify;">The separation occurs because smaller molecules pass through the pores of the gel matrix more easily than larger ones. The gel is sensitive to the physical size of the molecule. If the size of two fragments are similar or if they are of the same type of molecule, they will migrate at similar rates. If the size of the fragments are different, they will migrate at different rates. The migration rate of a molecule is inversely proportional to the log of its size in base pairs. This means that smaller molecules move faster through the gel matrix than larger molecules.</p>
    </div>

    <div style="margin-top: 30px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify;">Given two molecules of the same molecular weight and shape, the one with the greater amount of charge will migrate faster. In addition, differ-ent molecules can have different shapes, which affects their migration rate. Molecules that bind more strongly to the agarose will migrate more slowly.</p>
    </div>

    <div style="margin-top: 30px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify;">The mobility of molecules during electrophoresis is also influenced by gel concentration, buffer pH, and the applied voltage. The gel concentration and the volume to maximize successful experimental results.</p>
    </div>

    <div style="position: absolute; bottom: 30px; right: 60px; font-size: 10pt;">Page 7</div>
</div>

